    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most water heater leaks are not the tank. They are a loose connection on top, a relief valve doing its job, a drain valve that will not seal, or condensation that looks alarming and is not. The one leak you cannot fix is water coming from the steel tank itself, and the whole point of this guide is to tell those apart in ten minutes so you know whether you need a wrench or a new heater.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="first-steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What to Do in the First Ten Minutes
          </h2>

          <p>
            If water is pooling and you do not yet know why, make the heater safe before you start diagnosing. That means cutting the energy source first and the water second. Water and live electrical parts do not mix, and a gas burner that keeps firing while you drain a tank can overheat what is left inside. None of these steps damage anything, and all of them can be undone in a minute once you know the leak is minor.
          </p>

          <p>
            On an electric heater, switch off the double-pole breaker that feeds it at the panel. It is usually labelled, often a 30-amp breaker, and if it is not labelled, do not guess by flipping breakers while standing in water. On a gas heater, turn the gas control knob on the front of the valve to OFF, or to PILOT if you only want to stop the main burner. If the floor is wet around the control, close the gas shutoff on the supply line as well; the handle is perpendicular to the pipe when it is closed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, close the cold water supply valve on the pipe that enters the top of the heater. This stops the tank from refilling, so whatever is leaking can only lose what is already inside. Some older gate valves will not close fully, in which case the main shutoff for the house is the backup. If the valve handle is stiff, do not force it hard enough to crack the pipe; close the main instead and deal with the stuck valve later.
          </p>

          <p>
            With energy and water off, the leak will slow as pressure drops, especially if you open a hot water tap somewhere in the house to relieve it. If the leak stops completely, you very likely have a pressure-side leak at a fitting or valve. If it keeps seeping steadily from the bottom with the supply closed, suspect the tank. Only drain the heater if water is still escaping in a way that threatens floors or walls; otherwise, leave it full while you work out where the water is coming from.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The water inside can be 120 to 140°F or hotter.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Tank water hot enough to scald stays hot for hours after the burner or elements shut off. Wear gloves, keep your face away from the T&P valve and any loose fitting, and never open the drain valve into a hose you are holding by hand. If you smell gas at any point, leave the house and call the gas utility from outside.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="find-the-source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Find Where the Water Is Actually Coming From
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water runs down, so a puddle at the base tells you almost nothing about where the leak starts. Dry the floor, the outside of the jacket and every pipe you can reach with a towel. Then work from the top down with dry paper towels, pressing them against each fitting, the relief valve, the pipe below it and the drain valve. Wet paper shows you the source far faster than looking, because a slow drip runs along the jacket and drops off somewhere else entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the obvious non-heater sources while you are there. A water softener, a furnace or air conditioner condensate line, a washing machine hose, a leaking supply pipe in the ceiling above or a sweating cold water line can all drip onto the heater and make it look guilty. Follow any wet trail upward before you blame the tank. It is common for homeowners to replace a heater that had a leaking pipe joint above it, which is an expensive mistake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below maps the location of the leak to what usually causes it and how serious it is. Use it as the first sort, then jump to the section that matches. If you find water in more than one place, deal with the top first, because water from above can soak down through the insulation and appear at the bottom too.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where the water is</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Usual cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Top of tank, at the pipes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Loose or corroded connectors, dielectric fittings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fixable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">T&P valve or its discharge pipe</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High pressure, thermal expansion, worn valve</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fixable, find the cause</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drain valve at the base</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Worn washer, debris on seat, cracked plastic valve</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fixable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electric element access panels</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Element gasket leak</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fixable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under the tank, steady, no other source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Corroded inner tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replace</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Inside the burner area on a cold tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually condensation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often normal</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The condensation false alarm</h3>

          <p>
            Gas water heaters produce water vapour when they burn gas, and when the tank is full of cold water that vapour condenses on the cold steel of the flue and drips onto the burner. You may hear sizzling and find a small puddle under the heater, typically right after installation, after a big hot water draw or in humid weather. Manufacturers such as A.O. Smith and Rheem describe this in their manuals as normal, and it should stop once the tank reaches temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Condensation is worst on new heaters, oversized households that run the tank cold every morning, and heaters set at low temperatures. The tell is timing: it appears when the water is cold and fades as the tank heats up, and the water is clean rather than rusty. If water keeps appearing hours after the heater has recovered fully, or keeps coming when the burner is off, it is not condensation, and you should go back to the paper-towel test.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="top-leaks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Leaks From the Top: Fittings, Connectors and the Anode Port
          </h2>

          <p>
            The top of the tank is where most fixable leaks live. You have a cold inlet and a hot outlet, usually with flexible connectors or copper pipe running to the house, often with dielectric fittings that separate dissimilar metals. On gas models there is also the draft hood and flue, and on many tanks a hex-head plug that holds the anode rod. All of these are threaded or compression joints, and joints are what loosen, corrode and drip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the flexible connectors. Look for white or green crust around the nuts, rust on the nipples where they thread into the tank, and wet insulation at the base of each fitting. A nut that is weeping can often be stopped with a quarter turn while holding the fitting behind it with a second wrench so you do not twist the connector. If the connector is crusted, kinked or more than a decade old, replacement is cheap and quick: braided stainless water heater connectors are common hardware store items.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corroded nipples are the slower problem. The short steel or plastic-lined nipples that screw into the tank can rust through at the threads, especially where copper meets steel without proper isolation. A leak there shows up as rust stains running down from the port. Replacing a nipple means draining the tank below the top, unscrewing the old one and threading in a new dielectric nipple with thread sealant. It is a reasonable homeowner job, but if the threads in the tank itself are corroded, call a plumber before you make it worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            The anode port is the leak people miss. It sits on top of the tank, sometimes under a plastic cap or a layer of insulation, and the threads can weep after the rod has been changed or after years of corrosion. If water appears around a hex plug that is not connected to any pipe, that is likely the anode. Removing the rod, cleaning the threads and reinstalling with fresh thread sealant usually fixes it. Our guide to <a href="/water-heater-anode-rod-replacement/">water heater anode rod replacement</a> walks through the job step by step.
          </p>

          <p>
            One gas-specific caution: water dripping down the flue from above is sometimes rain or condensation coming down the vent, not the tank. Check the vent cap on the roof and look for staining on the vent pipe joints. A vent that lets water in can also be a vent that is not drafting properly, which is a combustion safety issue, so have a plumber or HVAC technician look at it rather than sealing it yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tp-valve-leaks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Water From the T&P Valve or Its Discharge Pipe
          </h2>

          <p>
            The temperature and pressure relief valve sits on the top or upper side of the tank with a pipe running down toward the floor. It is designed to open at 150 psi or 210°F, so water coming out of it is not a malfunction by default. It usually means the valve is responding to something real. The fix is finding that something, not stopping the valve, and the one thing you must never do is cap, plug or remove the discharge pipe.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common cause is thermal expansion. Water expands as it heats, and if your home has a check valve, backflow preventer or pressure-reducing valve at the meter, that expanded water has nowhere to go. Pressure spikes during each heating cycle and the valve weeps. The tell is a drip that shows up during or just after heating, not constantly. The usual fix is a properly sized and charged thermal expansion tank on the cold supply, which we cover in our guide to the <a href="/water-heater-expansion-tank/">water heater expansion tank</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second cause is high street pressure. Plumbing codes generally cap residential pressure at 80 psi and require a pressure-reducing valve above that. A hose-bib pressure gauge costs very little and tells you in seconds what your house is running at; check it at night too, when demand is low and pressure peaks. If it reads above 80 psi, the fix is a new or adjusted pressure-reducing valve, which also protects every fixture and appliance in the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            If pressure and expansion check out, the valve itself may be worn or have debris on its seat, which is common right after someone tests it on an older heater. Lifting the lever once or twice can flush the debris and let it reseat. If it keeps dripping, replace the valve with one of the same pressure and temperature rating and probe length. Our full guide to the <a href="/water-heater-pressure-relief-valve/">water heater pressure relief valve</a> covers testing, replacement and the discharge pipe rules.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">DO NOT DO THIS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never cap, plug or remove a dripping T&P valve.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The T&P valve is the only thing standing between an overheated, over-pressurised tank and an explosion. A drip is information. Find out why it is opening, or replace the valve, but never stop it from working.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="drain-valve-leaks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. A Dripping Drain Valve at the Base
          </h2>

          <p>
            The drain valve sits near the bottom of the tank, usually behind or below the lower access panel on an electric model and below the gas control on a gas model. Many factory valves are plastic, and they are a known weak point. They drip from the spout when the internal washer wears or sediment holds the seat open, and they can crack or leak around the stem if they get bumped or overtightened.
          </p>

          <p>
            If water drips from the spout, first make sure the valve is fully closed by turning it clockwise snugly but gently. Sediment on the seat is a common culprit, especially after someone has drained the tank. Opening the valve briefly with a hose attached to flush the debris, then closing it again, often stops the drip. If you do this, keep the hose end in a drain or outside and well away from your hands, because the water comes out hot and with force.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it still drips from the spout, a garden hose cap with a fresh rubber washer screwed onto the outlet is a legitimate short-term fix that many plumbers use. It stops the drip without touching the tank. For a permanent fix, the valve can be replaced with a brass full-port ball valve, which also makes future flushing much more effective because sediment passes through it rather than clogging a small plastic opening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacing the drain valve requires draining the tank completely, so pair it with a full flush. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater</a> covers the steps. If water is leaking around where the valve threads into the tank rather than from the spout, look closely: it may be the threads, which a new valve and sealant fix, or it may be tank corrosion around the opening, which they do not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tank-leaks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Water From the Bottom or the Tank Itself: Time to Replace
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you have dried everything, ruled out the top fittings, the T&P valve, the drain valve and condensation, and water is still seeping from under the tank, the inner steel tank has almost certainly corroded through. This is the end of the line. Glass-lined steel tanks cannot be patched in any meaningful way, because the leak is usually the first visible sign of corrosion that has been spreading for a while behind the insulation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other signs point the same way. Rust-coloured water at hot taps, rust stains on the jacket or floor, water pooling inside the burner compartment of a gas heater when it is fully hot, and a heater that is ten or more years old all strengthen the case. Tank failures often start as a seep and can progress to a sudden failure, so do not leave a confirmed tank leak running for weeks while you shop. If you are not sure how old yours is, our guide on <a href="/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/">how to tell the age of a water heater</a> shows how to read the serial number.
          </p>

          <p>
            On an electric heater, check the element access panels before condemning the tank. Remove the covers with the power off and look for water around the element. A leaking element gasket looks a lot like a tank leak from outside, and replacing the element and its gasket is an inexpensive job; see our guide to the <a href="/water-heater-element/">water heater element</a> for how to do it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once you have confirmed a tank leak, keep the energy source off, keep the cold supply closed and drain the tank to stop the seep. Then plan the replacement. Installed costs vary widely by fuel, size, venting and code updates, and our breakdown of <a href="/water-heater-replacement-cost/">water heater replacement cost</a> shows what drives the number. If you are choosing a new unit, our picks for the <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> are the place to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            A word on warranties. Most tank warranties run six to twelve years on the tank and cover a replacement heater, not the labour to install it. Check the warranty card or the manufacturer's website with your serial number before you buy anything, because a covered tank can cut the cost of the job substantially even when you pay for installation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pans-and-alarms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Drip Pans and Leak Alarms: Cheap Insurance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every tank water heater eventually leaks, and where it sits decides whether that leak is a nuisance or a flooded ceiling. A drain pan under the heater catches the water and, when it is piped to a floor drain or outside, carries it away. The International Plumbing Code requires a pan where a leak would cause damage, such as a heater in an attic, above a finished ceiling or on a wood floor, and many local codes add their own rules. Check what your jurisdiction requires before a replacement, because inspectors often look for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pans come in plastic and aluminium and in sizes to fit common tank diameters, typically a couple of inches wider than the heater. Codes generally call for a minimum depth and a drain line of at least three-quarters of an inch. A pan with no drain only holds a few gallons, which buys time for a slow seep but will not handle a failed tank. Gas heaters need a pan rated for the heat near the burner, which usually means metal or a plastic pan the manufacturer has approved for that use.
          </p>

          <p>
            A water leak alarm is the other half. A battery-powered sensor on the floor next to the heater, or inside the pan, sounds an alarm when it gets wet, and Wi-Fi models send an alert to your phone. That matters most when the heater is in a basement, garage or utility closet you do not visit every day. Our guide to <a href="/basement-water-alarm/">basement water alarms</a> covers what to look for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most complete version is an automatic shutoff valve. These install on the cold supply and close it when the sensor detects water, and some also shut the gas or power. They cost more and generally need a plumber, but for a heater above living space they turn a potential disaster into a damp pan. If you are replacing a leaking heater anyway, adding a pan, an alarm and an expansion tank where needed during the install costs relatively little and protects the next one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Draining the tank before finding the leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the leak is at a fitting, draining just removes the pressure that makes it visible, then you refill and have to start again. Shut off energy and water, dry everything and find the source with paper towels first. Only drain right away if water is escaping fast enough to damage the home.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Capping a dripping T&P valve</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A relief valve that drips is responding to high pressure, thermal expansion or a worn seat. Plugging it or capping the discharge pipe removes the only safety device that prevents an overheated tank from rupturing. Find the cause, fix the pressure or replace the valve, and leave the discharge path open.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the tank for a leak from above</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Condensate lines, softener drains, sweating pipes and leaking joints in the ceiling all drip onto water heaters. Follow the water trail upward before you buy anything. Homeowners regularly replace working heaters because a pipe joint above them was leaking, and the new heater gets wet too.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mistaking condensation for a tank failure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new gas heater, or any gas heater filled with cold water, can drip and sizzle as combustion vapour condenses on the cold flue. If the water appears when the tank is cold and stops once it has heated fully, it is condensation. Give it a day of normal use before deciding anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Restoring power to an empty electric tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you drained an electric heater, refill it completely and bleed air out at a hot tap before turning the breaker back on. Energising an element that is not under water burns it out within seconds. It is the most common and most avoidable way to turn a leak repair into an element replacement too.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a leaking water heater dangerous?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be. The main risks are water damage, scalding from hot water, and electrical hazards if water reaches wiring on an electric heater. A dripping T&P valve is a sign of excess pressure or temperature that needs attention. The tank itself is not likely to explode from a leak, but cut the energy source and close the supply until you know the cause.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I still use my water heater if it is leaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the leak is from a loose fitting and you have stopped it, yes. If the leak is from the T&P valve, find the cause first, because the valve is opening for a reason. If the tank itself is leaking, use it only as long as it takes to arrange a replacement, keep an eye on it, and drain it if the leak grows.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my water heater leaking from the bottom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The usual candidates are the drain valve, condensation on a gas model, water running down from a fitting on top, a T&P discharge pipe that ends near the floor, or a leaking element gasket on an electric model. If you rule all of those out and water still seeps from under the tank, the inner tank has corroded and needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much water leaking from a water heater is normal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A small amount of condensation under a gas heater when it is filled with cold water is normal and should stop once it heats up. Occasional drops from the T&P discharge pipe right after a heating cycle suggest thermal expansion. Anything steady, rusty or growing is not normal and needs to be traced.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will homeowners insurance cover a leaking water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Policies commonly cover sudden and accidental water damage from a failed heater but not the heater itself or damage from a slow leak that was ignored. Coverage varies by insurer and policy, so read yours and call your agent. Document the damage with photos, stop the leak promptly, and keep the failed heater until the claim is settled.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a water heater last after it starts leaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A leak from a fitting or valve says nothing about the tank's life and can be fixed. A leak from the tank itself means the steel has corroded through, and there is no reliable way to predict how long it will hold. Some seep for weeks; others fail suddenly. Treat a tank leak as a replacement you should schedule within days, not months.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I call a plumber for a leaking water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tightening a connector, capping a drain valve or flushing debris from a T&P valve is reasonable DIY. Call a plumber for gas line or venting work, a leak at corroded tank threads, a T&P valve that keeps opening, or any replacement. Many jurisdictions require a permit to replace a water heater, and a licensed installer handles it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

