        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A leaking water heater is a location problem before it is a repair problem. Cut the energy, close the cold supply, dry everything and find the exact source. Fittings, drain valves, element gaskets and most T&P drips are fixable for little money. A steady seep from the tank itself is not, and the right move is to drain it and plan the replacement.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you find, use it as a reason to add a drain pan and a leak alarm, and to check pressure and expansion if the relief valve was involved. If you are replacing the heater, our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best gas water heaters</a> guide is where we would start.
          </p>
